problems.reference_solution:
MOVES = {
    ('created', 'pay'): 'paid',
    ('paid', 'ship'): 'shipped',
    ('shipped', 'deliver'): 'delivered',
    ('created', 'cancel'): 'cancelled',
    ('paid', 'cancel'): 'cancelled',
}


class Order:
    def __init__(self):
        self._states = ['created']

    def state(self):
        return self._states[-1]

    def can(self, action):
        return (self.state(), action) in MOVES

    def apply(self, action):
        if not self.can(action):
            return False
        self._states.append(MOVES[self.state(), action])
        return True

    def history(self):
        return list(self._states)

questions.explanation:
Inheritance fits an "is a" relation. A car has an engine, so it should hold one. A stack
that inherits a list's methods lets callers break the stack's last-in first-out rule;
it should hold a list and expose only push and pop.

coin → unlocked; push → one person passes, locked; push → still locked; coin → unlocked;
coin → still unlocked. Every event has a defined result in every state, which is what
makes a state machine easy to check.
